  "query_preview": "..\nThe trade-wind trough that was forecast to bring scattered showers\nand a slight chance of thunderstorms today dissipated as the \ntemperature inversion and drier air aloft continued. However, an \napproaching trade-wind trough currently around 150E is much more \nprominent, with robust troughing apparent in the most recent \nscatterometry passes, and a line of scattered showers seen on \nvisible satellite. A ridge downstream will continue isolated light \nshowers across the region through Saturday morning before the trough \narrives Saturday afternoon, bringing scattered showers and strong \nlocalized gusts, similar to what was observed today at Saipan \nInternational Airport during a light rain shower. According to CIMSS \nMIMIC, Precipitable Water (PWAT) values associated with this trough \nare 2.0 to 2.5 inches, which highlights the uptick in moisture \ncompared to the surrounding drier air. Model time heights indicate \ndrier air aloft to continue, so thunderstorms are not a concern as of\nnow. This could change, though, depending on newer model runs. Once \nthe trough exits Tinian and Saipan Sunday and Guam and Rota Sunday \nafternoon, isolated showers may return through the rest of the \nforecast period. Gentle to moderate trade winds could increase to \nmoderate to fresh by the end of the weekend.\n\nAccording to the U.S. Drought Monitor, Guam is abnormally dry (D0) \nand the CNMI remains neutral. Water conservation efforts are \nencouraged as the dry season approaches, which could worsen drought \nconditions within the next several weeks.",
